[{:db/id "knowledge-point-05b40dd4-a99b-5d5e-93b4-767ed99d62a0" :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05b40dd4-a99b-5d5e-93b4-767ed99d62a0" :knowledge-point/canonical-example "question-4ced75f7-e2c9-50f2-b2cf-114222fc960d" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Two Events by Likelihood" :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e4735ca8-e709-5697-8aaf-f9b52ab3104d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186408190 :topic/knowledge-points ["knowledge-point-05b40dd4-a99b-5d5e-93b4-767ed99d62a0" "knowledge-point-05b40dd4-a99b-5d5e-93b4-767ed99d62a0"] :db/ensure [:topic/identity-validate]} {:db/id "question-4ced75f7-e2c9-50f2-b2cf-114222fc960d" :question/id #uuid "4ced75f7-e2c9-50f2-b2cf-114222fc960d" :question/math-academy-id "e-24942" :question/problem "A scientist records how long it takes for a chemical reaction to complete. The experiment is conducted $40$ times. The table below shows the results.\n\n| Time (seconds) | Frequency |\n| --- | --- |\n| $<10$ | $6$ |\n| $10-20$ | $14$ |\n| $20-30$ | $8$ |\n| $30-40$ | $7$ |\n| $>40$ | $5$ |\n\nBased on the experimental probabilities of these results, complete the following statements.\n\n$\\qquad$A reaction completing in more than $30$ seconds is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{\\text{unlikely}}}.$\n\n$\\qquad$A reaction completing in less than $20$ seconds is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{\\text{as likely as not}}}.$\n\n$\\qquad$A reaction completing in more than $30$ seconds is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{\\text{less likely than}}}$\na reaction completing in less than $20$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "One event is more likely than another if it has a greater probability of occurring. If two events have the same probability, they are equally likely.\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- To compute the experimental probability of a reaction completing in more than $30$ seconds, we divide the number of times a reaction took more than $30$ seconds $(7+5=12)$ by the total number of trials $(40).$ This gives\n![](images/41/41ae3b0ee5b77a5967ee949abfd5dc5327fc840e9061cecf1dfd52728405415f.svg)\n\nThis probability is a small number, closer to $0.$ Since\n\n$$\n0 < \\dfrac{3}{10} < \\dfrac12,\n$$\n\nthis event is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{unlikely}}.$\n\n- To compute the experimental probability of a reaction completing in less than $20$ seconds, we divide the number of times a reaction took less than $20$ seconds $(6+14=20)$ by the total number of trials $(40).$ This gives\n![](images/19/194542c8095442fe631a733e681d43b025ac5b5f7f478f7b3c541e3c51718c69.svg)\n\nSince the probability is $\\dfrac12,$ this event is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{as likely as not}}.$\n\n- The experimental probability of a reaction completing in more than $30$ seconds is less than the experimental probability of a reaction completing in less than $20$ seconds:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10} < \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a reaction completing in more than $30$ seconds is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{less likely than}}$\na reaction completing in less than $20$ seconds." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-e4735ca8-e709-5697-8aaf-f9b52ab3104d" :question/id #uuid "e4735ca8-e709-5697-8aaf-f9b52ab3104d" :question/math-academy-id "q-360663" :question/problem "Sarah conducts an experiment $40$ times to see how quickly a machine sorts a bin of parts. The table below shows the results of her experiments.\n\n| Time (seconds) | Frequency |\n| --- | --- |\n| $<20$ | $6$ |\n| $20-30$ | $16$ |\n| $30-40$ | $10$ |\n| $40-50$ | $5$ |\n| $>50$ | $3$ |\n\nBased on the experimental probabilities of these results, complete the following statements.\n\n$\\,$A machine sorting a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$A machine sorting a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$A machine sorting a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds is\n{{field-3}}\na machine sorting a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "One event is more likely than another if it has a greater probability of occurring. If two events have the same probability, they are equally likely.\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- To compute the experimental probability of a machine sorting a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds, we divide the number of times a machine sorted a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds $(6+16=22)$ by the total number of times Sarah conducted the experiment $(40).$ This gives\n\n$$\nP(\\text{sorts in }<30\\text{ seconds})=\\frac{22}{40}.\n$$\n\nThis probability is a relatively large number, closer to $1.$ Since\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}<\\frac{22}{40}<1,\n$$\n\nthis event is $\\text{likely}.$\n\n- To compute the experimental probability of a machine sorting a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds, we divide the number of times a machine sorted a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds $(10+5+3=18)$ by the total number of times Sarah conducted the experiment $(40).$ This gives\n\n$$\nP(\\text{sorts in }>30\\text{ seconds})=\\frac{18}{40}.\n$$\n\nThis probability is a small number, closer to $0.$ Since\n\n$$\n0<\\frac{18}{40}<\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nthis event is $\\text{unlikely}.$\n\n- The experimental probability of a machine sorting a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds is *greater than* the experimental probability of a machine sorting a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds:\n\n$$\nP(\\text{sorts in }<30\\text{ seconds})=\\frac{22}{40}>\\frac{18}{40}=P(\\text{sorts in }>30\\text{ seconds})\n$$\n\nTherefore, a machine sorting a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds is\n$\\text{more likely than}$\na machine sorting a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c257aad1-4038-59a0-af10-26f244b309a3" "answer-field-93f19fd1-92c4-5dd9-9417-4b37bc97723c" "answer-field-f45ede74-48fb-52d3-a7d6-85c98396d7b